      content: 'Key areas to assess for post-stroke dysphagia:\n- **Pre-stroke swallowing history**: Any prior dysphagia, GERD, esophageal stricture, or head/neck cancer? History of recurrent pneumonias?\n- **Current oral intake**: What textures and consistencies provoke symptoms? Thin liquids vs. solids? Is the patient avoiding certain foods?\n- **Aspiration signs**: Coughing or throat clearing during/after meals, wet or gurgly vocal quality after swallowing, recurrent fevers, unexplained desaturation.\n- **Nutritional status**: Pre-admission weight, current weight, serum albumin and prealbumin levels. Calorie count ordered?\n- **Hydration**: Urine output, BUN/Cr ratio, signs of clinical dehydration.\n- **Oral health**: Dentition status, oral hygiene, presence of oral thrush (poor oral hygiene increases aspiration pneumonia risk significantly even without frank aspiration).\n- **Cognition and neglect**: Left neglect may cause the patient to miss food on the left side of the tray, pocket food in the left buccal space, and have impaired awareness of swallowing difficulty.\n- **Medications**: Any sedating medications that could impair swallow coordination? Anticholinergics causing dry mouth?',
