      content: '**Videofluoroscopic Swallow Study (VFSS / Modified Barium Swallow)**:\n- **Procedure**: Patient swallows barium-coated food/liquid of various consistencies under fluoroscopy. Real-time lateral and AP views recorded.\n- **Strengths**: Visualizes all phases of swallowing (oral preparatory, oral transit, pharyngeal, upper esophageal). Can assess oral bolus control, hyoid/laryngeal excursion, UES opening, and esophageal clearance. Best for evaluating the **timing and coordination** of the entire swallow.\n- **Limitations**: Radiation exposure, requires transport to radiology, brief study (may miss intermittent aspiration), barium is not a normal food texture, cannot assess secretion management.\n- **Scales used**: **Penetration-Aspiration Scale (Rosenbek)** — 1 (no entry into airway) to 8 (silent aspiration, material enters airway and no effort to eject).\n\n**Fiberoptic Endoscopic Evaluation of Swallowing (FEES)**:\n- **Procedure**: Flexible laryngoscope passed transnasally to visualize the hypopharynx and larynx. Patient swallows food dyed with green food coloring.\n- **Strengths**: Portable (can be done at bedside — ideal for patients who cannot be transported). No radiation. Can assess **secretion management**, vocal fold function, and sensation (FEESST variant with air pulse). Can use real food. Can be repeated frequently to monitor progress.\n- **Limitations**: \'White-out\' phase during the pharyngeal swallow (cannot see the actual moment of swallow due to pharyngeal contraction). Cannot visualize the oral or esophageal phases. Cannot directly assess aspiration during the swallow — infers it from residue pre- and post-swallow.\n\n**Key distinction — Aspiration vs. Penetration**:\n- **Penetration**: Material enters the laryngeal vestibule but remains ABOVE the true vocal folds (PAS 2-5).\n- **Aspiration**: Material passes BELOW the true vocal folds into the trachea (PAS 6-8).\n- **Silent aspiration**: Aspiration without a cough reflex (PAS 8) — occurs in up to **40-70% of aspirating stroke patients** and is the most dangerous because it goes undetected clinically.\n\n**Recommendation for this patient**: I would order a **VFSS** as the initial study because:\n1. He needs a comprehensive assessment of all swallow phases — his bedside eval suggests both oral phase dysfunction (left-sided oral residue from neglect) and pharyngeal phase dysfunction (delayed trigger, penetration with thin liquids)\n2. We need to determine if compensatory strategies are effective (chin tuck, head turn to weaker side)\n3. He is stable enough for transport to radiology\n4. The results will guide specific diet texture recommendations and therapy targets',
