      content: '**A. Diet Modifications**:\n1. **Liquids**: IDDSI Level 2 (mildly thick) for all unmonitored intake. May trial thin liquids (IDDSI Level 0) with chin-tuck posture during supervised meals only.\n2. **Solids**: IDDSI Level 5 (minced and moist) to Level 6 (soft and bite-sized). Avoid mixed consistencies (e.g., cereal in milk, soup with chunks) as these are the highest-risk textures.\n3. **Calorie supplementation**: Add protein shakes (thickened to nectar consistency), calorie-dense foods (avocado, nut butters mixed into purees). Target >1500 kcal/day. Daily calorie counts for 1 week.\n4. **Hydration**: Track intake/output. Target 1500 mL/day. If unable to meet oral hydration needs, consider IV fluid supplementation.\n\n**B. Compensatory Strategies**:\n1. **Chin tuck**: During all liquid intake. Instruct patient to \'tuck your chin to your chest before and during the swallow.\'\n2. **Effortful swallow**: Bear down during the swallow to increase tongue base retraction and improve vallecular clearance.\n3. **Double swallow technique**: Swallow twice per bolus to clear pharyngeal residue.\n4. **Left cheek sweep**: After each bite, use the tongue or finger to sweep the left buccal sulcus to clear pocketed food (addresses neglect-related residue).\n5. **Upright positioning**: Sit at 90 degrees during meals and remain upright for **30 minutes** after eating.\n6. **Small bolus sizes**: Use a teaspoon (not tablespoon) for liquids. Take single sips rather than continuous drinking.\n7. **Reduce distractions**: Turn off TV, minimize conversation during meals to improve swallow attention.\n\n**C. Rehabilitative Swallowing Therapy (SLP-directed)**:\n1. **Lingual strengthening exercises**: Tongue press against palate, lateral tongue resistance with tongue depressor. Goal: improve tongue base retraction to reduce vallecular residue.\n2. **Mendelsohn maneuver**: Voluntarily hold the larynx in the elevated position during the swallow to prolong UES opening. Practice 10 repetitions, 3 times daily.\n3. **Shaker exercise (head lift)**: Supine head raises (sustained and repetitive) to strengthen suprahyoid muscles that open the UES.\n4. **Expiratory muscle strength training (EMST)**: Resistive expiratory device to improve cough strength and subglottic pressure.\n5. **Neuromuscular electrical stimulation (NMES)**: VitalStim or similar device to the submental/laryngeal muscles as adjunct during therapy. Evidence is mixed but may augment conventional therapy.\n\n**D. Oral Hygiene Protocol**:\n- Chlorhexidine oral rinse (0.12%) twice daily — **reduces aspiration pneumonia risk** even in patients who aspirate, by reducing oral bacterial load.\n- Supervised tooth brushing after every meal.\n- This is one of the most impactful and underutilized interventions for aspiration pneumonia prevention.\n\n**E. Monitoring and Reassessment**:\n- Weekly weight checks\n- Prealbumin levels every 2 weeks to track nutritional recovery\n- Repeat instrumental swallow study (FEES at bedside) in 2-3 weeks to assess for improvement and diet advancement\n- If no progress after 4 weeks, discuss nasogastric tube or PEG placement with patient and family',
